1
00:00:02,850 --> 00:00:08,048
An ambulance has to cross Bengaluru. Which road is fastest, right now?

2
00:00:09,583 --> 00:00:14,548
This is Corridor: a working prototype that routes ambulances across Bengaluru.

3
00:00:14,668 --> 00:00:19,846
The board is live. Every ambulance is driving a route solved a moment ago.

4
00:00:19,966 --> 00:00:27,379
832 junctions, 1,630 roads, 15 hospitals, and five algorithms to compare.

5
00:00:29,967 --> 00:00:33,450
The problem: the fastest road is rarely the shortest one.

6
00:00:33,867 --> 00:00:38,561
A city is a graph. Each road costs its travel time this minute.

7
00:00:39,267 --> 00:00:45,909
The chosen algorithms: A* for a trip between two places, multi-target Dijkstra for the nearest hospital.

8
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:55,357
Story one. 6:30 pm, a call from Yeshwanthpur. Which hospital, and which roads?

9
00:00:55,477 --> 00:01:01,624
One multi-target Dijkstra spreads from the incident and stops at the first hospital it reaches.

10
00:01:01,744 --> 00:01:05,574
The ticket: 26.2 minutes to ESI Rajajinagar.

11
00:01:05,694 --> 00:01:09,626
It searched only 15 of 832 junctions.

12
00:01:09,994 --> 00:01:15,167
Ramaiah Memorial is closer on the map, but would take 33.7 minutes.

13
00:01:15,287 --> 00:01:17,124
Distance is the wrong question.

14
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:25,615
Any trip works. Sixty-two real places: just type a few letters.

15
00:01:25,735 --> 00:01:29,873
Kengeri to Whitefield, right across the city at the evening peak.

16
00:01:30,435 --> 00:01:34,776
A* aims its search at the destination instead of flooding the map.

17
00:01:35,635 --> 00:01:40,046
The same answer as Dijkstra, with 46% of the work.

18
00:01:44,133 --> 00:01:50,202
Now change the world. One tap sets the scene: rush hour, rain, a lazy Sunday.

19
00:01:50,322 --> 00:01:54,562
The panel explains the slowdown: time of day, weather and events.

20
00:01:54,822 --> 00:01:57,956
Drag the clock, and every road is re-priced.

21
00:02:00,022 --> 00:02:05,979
Even an apocalypse: 123 roads closed, the city nearly five times slower.

22
00:02:06,099 --> 00:02:08,248
The dispatch re-plans at once.

23
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:17,736
Story three. A monsoon flood shuts 28 roads between Electronic City and Hebbal.

24
00:02:17,856 --> 00:02:23,488
A* routes around the water. A closed road is simply a cost of infinity.

25
00:02:23,856 --> 00:02:31,046
The ticket splits the 134 minutes: 38 of open road, 70 of traffic, 27 of rain.

26
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:34,626
The detour itself costs only 3.7 minutes.

27
00:02:37,267 --> 00:02:40,104
Big picture opens any search full screen.

28
00:02:41,267 --> 00:02:44,258
Watch A* lean north, towards Hebbal.

29
00:02:45,667 --> 00:02:51,177
281 junctions and 1,098 road segments to find the route.

30
00:02:51,297 --> 00:02:57,042
Bellman–Ford finds the same route after 112,140 edge checks.

31
00:02:57,162 --> 00:02:59,607
Drag the scrubber to walk through the search.

32
00:03:04,067 --> 00:03:09,977
The race. Five algorithms, one trip. The clock counts edges examined.

33
00:03:10,097 --> 00:03:14,587
Greedy sprints home first, but its route is 47% slower.

34
00:03:14,707 --> 00:03:17,813
Among the correct ones, A* finishes first.

35
00:03:18,907 --> 00:03:23,168
Bellman–Ford is still re-reading the whole map, so skip to the end.

36
00:03:23,288 --> 00:03:30,324
136,920 edges, for the answer A* found with 1,271.

37
00:03:30,444 --> 00:03:35,325
Four optimal algorithms, one answer. Only the work differs.

38
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:43,673
Does it actually work? Ten executable tests run the real algorithms in the browser.

39
00:03:43,793 --> 00:03:47,565
Optimality, edge cases, and a heuristic that never lies.

40
00:03:49,093 --> 00:03:51,014
Verified: ten out of ten.

41
00:03:55,300 --> 00:04:00,735
The benchmark lab measures complexity: eight cities, up to 8,000 junctions.

42
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:08,188
Dijkstra and A* grow almost linearly. Bellman–Ford does 189 times the work.

43
00:04:08,308 --> 00:04:13,827
On the largest city, A* examines 58% of the edges Dijkstra does.

44
00:04:14,708 --> 00:04:23,486
The honest limit: A* needs 35% of the search Dijkstra does at 3 am, but 57% at the evening peak.

45
00:04:23,606 --> 00:04:25,918
Every number here was measured live.

46
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:37,413
The verdict. A* is optimal, needs no preprocessing, and survives weights that change every minute.

47
00:04:37,533 --> 00:04:42,925
Bellman–Ford pays O(V·E) for negative edges that travel time never has.

48
00:04:43,045 --> 00:04:47,347
Dispatch is a different question, so it gets multi-target Dijkstra.

49
00:04:50,333 --> 00:04:54,364
One more thing: a night chart, for the 3 am shift.

50
00:04:54,933 --> 00:05:00,743
Airport to Electronic City on empty roads: 59 km in 66 minutes.

51
00:05:10,302 --> 00:05:16,401
Corridor. Built by Nebin Stanly. Open it, and try the stories yourself.

